# dyn/settle - the settle: a system's populations relaxed together, sweep after sweep, until every unit's residual is
# within the tolerance (docs/settling.md 1.2). The Engine holds what a planned system runs on - its populations with
# their named state as Columns of cells, its connections, the flat parameter region and every workspace - all laid out
# once, so a sweep, a settle and a lesson allocate nothing.
#
# A sweep (Jacobi). Every population's state is double-buffered: a sweep reads buffer k and writes buffer 1 - k, so
# every population can relax in a task of its own at once. A sweep is cut into units - one population's rows of one
# phase, in chunks of at most Chunk rows - and the units go to the system's tasks; a unit computes its rows' totals
# (the constant, every connection's carry of the activities read, the nudge of a nudged readout), their residual and
# their step. The chunks do not depend on the thread count and every element's sums run in one order, so a settle is
# the same, bit for bit, for any number of threads.
#
# The constant. Inputs are not relaxed: their state is set from their drive in closed form at the start of a settle,
# and every connection out of an input is carried once then, into a constant per batch row (drive + bias + the folded
# carries) that each sweep starts from.
#
# Phases side by side. A population's columns are phases x batch x size long: the free phase's rows, then each nudged
# phase's. The free phase settles first; the nudged phases start from its fixed point and settle together (Nudged), one
# product per connection per sweep for both. Between settles the committed state is buffer 0 (a settle that ends on
# buffer 1 copies its rows back).
#
# Gauss-Seidel sweeps (Sweeping.GaussSeidel): the populations are swept one after another in their sweep order (a
# topological order of the one-way connections, so a chain is carried through in one sweep), each reading the state
# the sweep already wrote for the populations before it and the state it started from for the rest (itself included:
# a population's rows still relax as one Jacobi block, in tasks as above). Where the system is certified (kappa < 1)
# the sweep contracts in the sup norm at least as a Jacobi one does, in any order; a spiking system's sweep is a time
# step, and stays Jacobi. A population's residual is measured against totals partly new, so a Gauss-Seidel residual
# is the step each unit made, and the state kept is the one the converged sweep wrote.
#
# Anderson acceleration (Sweeping.Anderson = m, Walker and Ni 2011's type II): after a sweep the next state is the
# combination of the last m + 1 sweeps' states and steps whose steps' combination is smallest in the least-squares
# sense - x + f - (dX + dF) gamma, gamma = argmin |f - dF gamma| - rather than x + f. The residual is still measured
# by the sweep, of the state it read; where it grows the history is dropped and the plain step taken (the safeguard).
# Rate populations only: a system with a spiking or custom population sweeps plainly. Its room is reserved (Reserve)
# for a depth up to Depth. A learner's exact adjoint is accelerated the same way, by the same setting (Accelerate).
#
# A step is v += dt r, dt the system's (1: plain fixed-point iteration) - smaller, dt / (1 + beta lambda), in a phase
# nudged toward its targets (settling.md 1.2: the nudge then only damps) - times 1 - Damping. A spiking system's sweep
# is a time step and its residual the largest change of a rate over a window, measured at the window's end.

import "std/rand"
import "../tensor"
import "../kernels/gemm"
import "../kernels/sparse"
import "neuron"
import "conn"
import "halt"

# the rows of a unit of work, at most
Chunk I64 = 128

# the phases a system holds side by side: free, nudged toward the targets, nudged away (centered EP)
type Phase enum { Free, Plus, Minus }

fn (p Phase) Index() I64 {
    return match p {
        case Phase.Free: 0
        case Phase.Plus: 1
        case Phase.Minus: 2
    }
}

# how a settle sweeps: Jacobi (the default) or Gauss-Seidel sweeps, accelerated by Anderson's method of depth Anderson
# (0: none; at most the depth reserved, Engine.Reserve)
type Sweeping struct(Anderson I64 = 0, GaussSeidel Bool = false) {
    Anderson
    GaussSeidel
}

# what a settle may take: at most Sweeps sweeps (time steps, spiking), stopping when every residual is at most Tol; a
# step shortened by the factor 1 - Damping; the sweeps of Scheme. Euclid: the residual tested is each row's Euclidean
# norm over its relaxing units (the norm of the spectral certificate), not each unit's; a rate system of built-in
# models only (a spiking or custom one is tested unit by unit). Stable (k > 0): a row also stops once its answer - the
# class each readout group's largest unit names - has been the same for k sweeps (dyn/halt), and the settle once every
# row has stopped either way; a rate system on the CPU
type Budget struct(Sweeps I64 = 128, Tol F64 = 1.25e-3, Damping F64 = 0, Scheme Sweeping = Sweeping(), Euclid Bool = false, Stable I64 = 0) {
    Sweeps
    Tol
    Damping
    Scheme
    Euclid
    Stable
}

# how a settle went: the sweeps it took, the residual's norm at the end (the sup norm, or the largest row's Euclidean
# norm under Budget.Euclid) (NaN or infinite where it diverged), and
# whether it is within the tolerance; Halted: it stopped on stable answers (Budget.Stable) before the tolerance
type Settlement struct(Sweeps I64 = 0, Residual F64 = 0, Converged Bool = false, Halted Bool = false) {
    Sweeps
    Residual
    Converged
    Halted
}

# whether the settle diverged: a residual that is not finite
fn (s Settlement&) Diverged() Bool { return not (s.Residual <= F64.Max) }

# ---- a population as the settle runs it ----

# size units of model in role, their state in rows rows (phases x batch): a rate (or custom) population's cells in R0
# and R1, a spiking one's in L0 and L1; Total its totals, Const its constant per batch row, Drive what was clamped,
# Target and Labels a readout's, Force a spiking readout's nudge, Alive which units are not retired, Bias where its
# biases are in the parameter region, Unit where its units are among all of the system's. In, Back and Folds are the
# connections into it: carried forward, carried back (reciprocal, it their pre side), and folded into the constant
type Population<T> struct(size I64, model neuron.Neuron, role neuron.Role, rows I64, batch I64) {
    Size I64 = size
    Model neuron.Neuron = model
    Role neuron.Role = role
    Spiking Bool = model == neuron.Neuron.Lif
    R0 Columns<neuron.RateCell<T>> = Columns<neuron.RateCell<T>>(0 if Spiking else rows * size)
    R1 Columns<neuron.RateCell<T>> = Columns<neuron.RateCell<T>>(0 if Spiking else rows * size)
    L0 Columns<neuron.LifCell<T>> = Columns<neuron.LifCell<T>>(rows * size if Spiking else 0)
    L1 Columns<neuron.LifCell<T>> = Columns<neuron.LifCell<T>>(rows * size if Spiking else 0)
    Total mut Array<T>& = Array<T>(rows * size)
    Const mut Array<T>& = Array<T>(batch * size)
    Drive mut Array<T>& = Array<T>(batch * size)
    Target mut Array<T>& = Array<T>(batch * size if role == neuron.Role.Readout else 0)
    Labels mut Array<I32>& = Array<I32>(batch * GroupsOf(role))
    Force mut Array<T>& = Array<T>(rows * size if Spiking and role == neuron.Role.Readout else 0)
    Alive mut Array<Bool>& = Array<Bool>(size, true)
    Retired I64 = 0
    Bias I64 = 0
    Unit I64 = 0
    In mut Vec<I64>& = Vec<I64>()
    Back mut Vec<I64>& = Vec<I64>()
    Folds mut Vec<I64>& = Vec<I64>()
}

# a readout's groups (one class per group a row), none for other roles
fn GroupsOf(role neuron.Role) I64 {
    if role != neuron.Role.Readout: return 0
    return match role.cost {
        case neuron.Cost.CrossEntropy(_, groups): groups
        case neuron.Cost.Squared: 0
    }
}

fn (p mut Population<T>&) rate(k I64) mut Columns<neuron.RateCell<T>>& { return p.R0 if k == 0 else p.R1 }

fn (p mut Population<T>&) rateS() mut Array<T>& { return p.R0.S }

fn (p mut Population<T>&) lif(k I64) mut Columns<neuron.LifCell<T>>& { return p.L0 if k == 0 else p.L1 }

# the activities the connections out of it carry, in buffer k: rate activities, or a spiking population's spikes
fn (p mut Population<T>&) carried(k I64) mut Array<T>& {
    if p.Spiking: return p.lif(k).Spike
    return p.rate(k).S
}

# the rate activities in buffer k, or the spiking population's trace: what a nudged readout is pulled from
fn (p mut Population<T>&) readout(k I64) mut Array<T>& {
    if p.Spiking: return p.lif(k).Trace
    return p.rate(k).S
}

# its activities in buffer k
fn (p mut Population<T>&) Activity(k I64) mut Array<T>& {
    if p.Spiking: return p.lif(k).S
    return p.rate(k).S
}

# ---- the engine ----

# a planned system's running part: batch rows a phase, phases of them, sweeps split over threads tasks
type Engine<T> struct(batch I64, phases I64, threads I64) {
    Batch I64 = batch
    Phases I64 = phases
    Threads I64 = threads if threads > 0 else 1
    Rows I64 = batch * phases
    Pops mut Vec<mut Population<T>&>& = Vec<mut Population<T>&>()
    Links mut Vec<mut conn.Link<T>&>& = Vec<mut conn.Link<T>&>()
    Params mut Array<T>& = Array<T>(0)
    Grads mut Array<T>& = Array<T>(0)
    Beta mut Array<F64>& = Array<F64>(phases)       # each phase's nudge
    Dt F64 = 1.0                                    # a sweep's step, in (0, 1]
    Spiking Bool = false
    Window I64 = 1
    Units I64 = 0                                   # every population's units
    TaskWork I64 = 2000000                          # the multiply-adds a sweep needs to be split over tasks
    Sweeps I64 = 0                                  # every sweep of every settle
    Last Settlement = Settlement()
    cur I64 = 0                                     # the buffer a sweep reads
    sweep I64 = 0                                   # the sweep (time step) running
    damping F64 = 0.0                               # the running settle's
    ws mut Vec<mut gemm.Workspace<T>&>& = Vec<mut gemm.Workspace<T>&>()
    wide mut gemm.Workspace<T>& = gemm.Workspace<T>(threads)   # the constants' products, whole batches, split by gemm
    wideSc mut conn.Scratch<T>& = conn.Scratch<T>(0, 0, 0)
    sc mut Vec<mut conn.Scratch<T>&>& = Vec<mut conn.Scratch<T>&>()
    units mut Array<I64>& = Array<I64>(0)           # a settle's units: population, first row, end row
    work I64 = 0                                    # a sweep's multiply-adds, about
    count I64 = 0
    worst mut Array<T>& = Array<T>(0)               # each unit's residual
    Mass mut Array<F64>& = Array<F64>(0)             # each unit's incoming mass (the gain)
    order mut Array<I64>& = Array<I64>(0)           # the populations in sweep order
    groups mut Array<I64>& = Array<I64>(0)          # the first unit of each population in that order, and the end
    fresh mut Array<Bool>& = Array<Bool>(0)         # Gauss-Seidel: whether population i was swept in this sweep
    seidel Bool = false                             # the running settle's sweeps are Gauss-Seidel
    PackAhead Bool = true                           # a settle long enough packs its dense weights once, not in every sweep (conn.Link.Repack)
    RowStops Bool = true                            # rows stop on their own residual where a settle allows it (mayStop); false: every row sweeps until the last is in
    RowDone mut Array<Bool>& = Array<Bool>(phases * batch)     # the stoppable rows that have stopped in the running settle
    RowStop mut Array<I64>& = Array<I64>(phases * batch)       # the sweep each row stopped at in the last settle (-1: it ran to the end)
    RowRes mut Array<F64>& = Array<F64>(phases * batch)        # each row's residual after the last sweep of a rate settle on rows
    stopping Bool = false                           # the running settle's rows may stop on their own
    idle I64 = 0                                    # the row-sweeps of the planned units' populations left out, rows stopped
    Skipped I64 = 0                                 # every population's row-sweeps not run because their rows had stopped
    acc mut History<T>& = History<T>(0, 0)        # Anderson's history
    answers mut halt.Stable& = halt.Stable(0, 1)  # each row's answer, its readouts' groups' classes (Budget.Stable)
    spec mut Vec<mut Array<T>&>& = Vec<mut Array<T>&>()    # Spectral's vector (zero until drawn), kept to warm-start the next call
    specW mut Vec<mut Array<T>&>& = Vec<mut Array<T>&>()   # its product with the coupling
}

# Anderson's history: the state a sweep read (X) and the step it made (F), gathered over the rows settled, the last ones
# (PX, PF), Depth columns of their differences (DX, DF) and the differences' Gram matrix, Kept of them in use; Slot the
# column written next; Last the last sweep's residual
type History<T> struct(size I64, depth I64) {
    Depth I64 = depth
    X mut Array<T>& = Array<T>(size)
    F mut Array<T>& = Array<T>(size)
    PX mut Array<T>& = Array<T>(size)
    PF mut Array<T>& = Array<T>(size)
    DX mut Array<T>& = Array<T>(depth * size)
    DF mut Array<T>& = Array<T>(depth * size)
    Gram mut Array<F64>& = Array<F64>(depth * depth)
    A mut Array<F64>& = Array<F64>(depth * depth)
    G mut Array<F64>& = Array<F64>(depth)
    Kept I64 = 0
    Slot I64 = 0
    Started Bool = false
    Last F64 = 0.0
}

# the population numbered i, and connection i
fn (e Engine<T>&) Pop(i I64) mut Population<T>& { return e.Pops[i] }

fn (e Engine<T>&) Link(i I64) mut conn.Link<T>& { return e.Links[i] }

# the work room, once the populations and connections are recorded and laid out: a workspace and a scratch per task,
# the units a settle can have, the masses
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) Ready() {
    widest I64 = 1
    rank I64 = 1
    for p in e.Pops: widest = widest.Max(p.Size)
    for l in e.Links {
        kind := l.Kind
        if kind == conn.Wiring.LowRank: rank = rank.Max(kind.rank)
    }
    units I64 = 0
    for p in e.Pops: units += p.Size
    e.Units = units
    room I64 = widest.Max(rank).Max(e.Rows).Max(e.Batch.Max(Chunk))   # every product's sizes: units, rows, rank
    e.wide.Reserve(room)
    for _ in range e.Threads {
        e.ws.Push(gemm.Workspace<T>(1, room))
        sc := conn.Scratch<T>(widest, Chunk, rank)
        for l in e.Links {
            if l.Kind == conn.Wiring.Events: sc.Ev.Push(sparse.Events(l.PreSize))
        }
        e.sc.Push(sc)
    }
    e.wideSc = conn.Scratch<T>(widest, e.Batch.Max(Chunk), rank)
    for l in e.Links: l.Prepack()
    for l in e.Links {
        if l.Kind != conn.Wiring.Events: continue
        l.Index = e.wideSc.Ev.Len()                 # its collector in every scratch
        e.wideSc.Ev.Push(sparse.Events(l.PreSize))
    }
    most := e.Pops.Len() * e.Phases * ((e.Batch + Chunk - 1) / Chunk + (e.Batch + 1) / 2)
    e.units = Array<I64>(3 * most)
    e.worst = Array<T>(most)
    e.Mass = Array<F64>(units)
    e.Spiking = false
    for p in e.Pops {
        model := p.Model
        if model != neuron.Neuron.Lif: continue
        e.Spiking = true
        e.Window = model.window
    }
    e.sweepOrder()
    width I64 = 0
    for p in e.Pops {
        if p.Role == neuron.Role.Readout: width += GroupsOf(p.Role).Max(1)
    }
    e.answers = halt.Stable(e.Rows, 1, width)
    e.groups = Array<I64>(e.Pops.Len() + 1)
    e.fresh = Array<Bool>(e.Pops.Len())
    for p in e.Pops {
        e.spec.Push(Array<T>(p.Size))
        e.specW.Push(Array<T>(p.Size))
    }
}

# the populations in sweep order: each after every population a one-way connection carries into it from (a
# topological order; a reciprocal connection's way back and a lateral block do not order), where a cycle of them
# leaves none ready, the lowest numbered left; else in the order they were made
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) sweepOrder() {
    n := e.Pops.Len()
    need := Array<I64>(n)
    for l in e.Links {
        if not l.Folded and l.Pre != l.Post: need[l.Post]++
    }
    done := Array<Bool>(n)
    e.order = Array<I64>(n)
    for at in range n {
        pick := firstReady(need, done)
        done[pick] = true
        e.order[at] = pick
        for l in e.Links {
            if not l.Folded and l.Pre == pick and l.Post != pick: need[l.Post]--
        }
    }
}

# the first population not yet placed that nothing unplaced feeds, or the first not yet placed
fn firstReady(need Array<I64>&, done Array<Bool>&) I64 {
    for i in range need.Len() {
        if not done[i] and need[i] <= 0: return i
    }
    for i in range need.Len() {
        if not done[i]: return i
    }
    unreachable "every population is placed"
}

# room for Anderson acceleration up to depth: the history of every relaxing population's rows, every phase (a
# learner's adjoint, Accelerate, needs one phase's); it allocates, once, where the caller chooses
# (a smaller depth than reserved needs nothing)
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) Reserve(depth I64) {
    if depth <= e.acc.Depth: return
    size I64 = 0
    for p in e.Pops {
        if p.Role != neuron.Role.Input and not p.Spiking: size += e.Rows * p.Size
    }
    e.acc = History<T>(size, depth)
}

# ---- state between settles ----

# whether a cell type keeps a field across settles: the field is a case of its member Kept
fn kept<C>(name String&) Bool {
    if not C.HasMember("Kept"): return false
    for c in C.Kept.Cases {
        if c.Name == name: return true
    }
    return false
}

# a cell type's columns: rows lo .. hi of every field but the kept ones (its Kept) set to zero
fn clearRows<C>(c mut Columns<C>&, lo I64, hi I64) {
    for k, f in C.Fields {
        if kept<C>(f.Name): continue
        z f.Type
        for i in range lo, hi: Columns<C>.Fields[k].Of(c)[i] = z
    }
}

# rows from .. from + n of a's columns into rows to .. of b's, every field
fn copyRows<C>(a Columns<C>&, b mut Columns<C>&, from I64, to I64, n I64) {
    for k, _ in C.Fields {
        for i in range n: Columns<C>.Fields[k].Of(b)[to + i] = Columns<C>.Fields[k].Of(a)[from + i]
    }
}

# phase ph's state set to rest (every field but the kept ones zero): a cold start. Inputs are left: a settle sets
# theirs from their drive
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) Reset(ph I64) {
    for p in e.Pops {
        if p.Role == neuron.Role.Input: continue
        lo := ph * e.Batch * p.Size
        hi := lo + e.Batch * p.Size
        if p.Spiking { clearRows(p.L0, lo, hi) } else { clearRows(p.R0, lo, hi) }
    }
}

# phase to's state set to phase from's (a nudged phase starts from the free fixed point). Inputs are left: a settle
# sets theirs from their drive
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) Warm(to I64, from I64) {
    if to == from: return
    for p in e.Pops {
        if p.Role == neuron.Role.Input: continue
        n := e.Batch * p.Size
        if p.Spiking { copyRows(p.L0, p.L0, from * n, to * n, n) } else { copyRows(p.R0, p.R0, from * n, to * n, n) }
    }
}

# rows lo .. hi of buffer 1 copied into buffer 0, every population: where a settle ending on buffer 1 commits
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) commit(r0 I64, r1 I64) {
    for p in e.Pops {
        if p.Role == neuron.Role.Input: continue
        n := (r1 - r0) * p.Size
        if p.Spiking { copyRows(p.L1, p.L0, r0 * p.Size, r0 * p.Size, n) } else { copyRows(p.R1, p.R0, r0 * p.Size, r0 * p.Size, n) }
    }
}

# ---- the start of a settle ----

# phases lo .. hi made ready: every input's state set from its drive in closed form (in buffer 0: only folded
# connections, run from there, read an input); every relaxing
# population's constant made (with fold: its drive, its bias and the folded carries of its inputs); the counts of a
# spiking population's window cleared; event-driven connections forget what they carried
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) prepare(lo I64, hi I64, fold Bool) {
    r0, r1 := lo * e.Batch, hi * e.Batch
    for p in e.Pops {
        if p.Role == neuron.Role.Input: e.closedForm(p, r0, r1)
        if p.Spiking: e.clearCounts(p, r0, r1)
    }
    for l in e.Links: l.Forget(r0, r1)
    if not fold: return
    for p in e.Pops {
        if p.Role != neuron.Role.Input: e.constant(e.wide, e.wideSc, p, 0, e.Batch)
    }
}

# an input's rows r0 .. r1 (whole phases), in buffer 0: U = drive, S = rho(drive)
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) closedForm(p mut Population<T>&, r0 I64, r1 I64) {
    model := p.Model
    rho := model.rho if model == neuron.Neuron.Rate else neuron.Rho.Linear
    n := e.Batch * p.Size
    for at := r0 * p.Size, at < r1 * p.Size, at += n {
        for i in range n: p.R0.U[at + i] = p.Drive[i]
        neuron.ActivateRun(rho, p.R0.U, p.R0.S, at, at + n)
    }
}

fn (e mut Engine<T>&) clearCounts(p mut Population<T>&, r0 I64, r1 I64) {
    for i in range r0 * p.Size, r1 * p.Size {
        p.L0.Count[i] = T(0)
        p.L1.Count[i] = T(0)
    }
}

# ---- units of work ----

# the units of phases lo .. hi: every relaxing population's live rows of each (all of them unless rows have stopped,
# RowDone), as runs of consecutive rows of at most Chunk, the populations in sweep order - the stopped rows are left out
# of the products, which run over the live rows only
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) plan(lo I64, hi I64) {
    e.count = 0
    e.work = 0
    e.idle = 0
    for at in range e.Pops.Len() {
        i := e.order[at]
        p := e.Pops[i]
        e.groups[at] = e.count
        if p.Role == neuron.Role.Input: continue
        carried := p.Size
        for l in p.In: carried += e.Links[l].Count
        for l in p.Back: carried += e.Links[l].Count
        live := 0
        for ph in range lo, hi: live += e.runs(i, ph * e.Batch, (ph + 1) * e.Batch)
        e.work += live * carried
        e.idle += (hi - lo) * e.Batch - live
    }
    e.groups[e.Pops.Len()] = e.count
}

# population i's units over rows r0 .. r1 of one phase: each run of consecutive live rows, cut at Chunk rows; the live
# rows counted
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) runs(i I64, r0 I64, r1 I64) I64 {
    live := 0
    r := r0
    for r < r1 {
        if e.stopping and e.RowDone[r] {
            r++
            continue
        }
        end := r + 1
        for end < r1 and end - r < Chunk and not (e.stopping and e.RowDone[end]): end++
        e.units[3 * e.count] = i
        e.units[3 * e.count + 1] = r
        e.units[3 * e.count + 2] = end
        e.count++
        live += end - r
        r = end
    }
    return live
}

# a sweep: every unit at once (Jacobi), or each population's units after the last's (Gauss-Seidel); the largest
# residual (NaN when one is)
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) sweepOnce() T {
    if not e.seidel: return e.run(0, e.count)
    worst T = T(0)
    for at in range e.Pops.Len() {
        worst = neuron.Worse(worst, e.run(e.groups[at], e.groups[at + 1]))
        e.fresh[e.order[at]] = true
    }
    for i in range e.fresh.Len(): e.fresh[i] = false
    return worst
}

# units u0 .. u1 run, on the system's tasks - each task the units u0 + t, u0 + t + tasks, ... - and their largest
# residual. A sweep too small to pay for tasks runs on the calling thread; the units are the same either way
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) run(u0 I64, u1 I64) T {
    tasks := e.Threads.Min(u1 - u0)
    if e.work < e.TaskWork: tasks = 1
    if tasks <= 1 {
        e.task(u0, u1, 0, 1)
    } else {
        join {
            for t in range tasks: spawn e.task(u0, u1, t, tasks)
        }
    }
    worst T = T(0)
    for u in range u0, u1: worst = neuron.Worse(worst, e.worst[u])
    return worst
}

fn (e mut Engine<T>&) task(u0 I64, u1 I64, t I64, every I64) {
    ws := e.ws[t]
    sc := e.sc[t]
    for u in range u0 + t, u1, every {
        p := e.Pops[e.units[3 * u]]
        r0, r1 := e.units[3 * u + 1], e.units[3 * u + 2]
        e.totals(ws, sc, p, r0, r1)
        e.worst[u] = e.lifStep(p, r0, r1) if p.Spiking else e.rateStep(p, r0, r1)
    }
}

# batch rows r0 .. r1 of p's constant: drive + bias + every folded connection's carry of its input's activities
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) constant(ws mut gemm.Workspace<T>&, sc mut conn.Scratch<T>&, p mut Population<T>&, r0 I64, r1 I64) {
    n := p.Size
    for r in range r0, r1 {
        for j in range n: p.Const[r * n + j] = p.Drive[r * n + j] + e.Params[p.Bias + j]
    }
    for i in p.Folds {
        l := e.Links[i]
        pre := e.Pops[l.Pre].rateS()
        l.Forward(ws, sc, p.Const, pre, r0, r1)
    }
}

# rows r0 .. r1 of p's totals from buffer cur: the constant (the drive and the folded inputs; nothing for a spiking
# population, whose constant enters at the membrane), every connection's carry, and a nudged readout's force
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) totals(ws mut gemm.Workspace<T>&, sc mut conn.Scratch<T>&, p mut Population<T>&, r0 I64, r1 I64) {
    n := p.Size
    k := e.cur
    for r in range r0, r1 {
        b := r % e.Batch
        for j in range n: p.Total[r * n + j] = T(0) if p.Spiking else p.Const[b * n + j]
    }
    for i in p.In {
        l := e.Links[i]
        pre := e.Pops[l.Pre].carried(e.bufferOf(l.Pre))
        l.Forward(ws, sc, p.Total, pre, r0, r1)
    }
    for i in p.Back {
        l := e.Links[i]
        post := e.Pops[l.Post].carried(e.bufferOf(l.Post))
        l.Backward(ws, sc, p.Total, post, r0, r1)
    }
    beta := e.Beta[r0 / e.Batch]
    role := p.Role
    if beta == 0.0 or role != neuron.Role.Readout: return
    into := p.Force if p.Spiking else p.Total
    from := p.readout(k)
    for r in range r0, r1 {
        b := r % e.Batch
        if p.Spiking { for j in range n: into[r * n + j] = T(0) }
        neuron.AddForce(role.cost, from, r * n, n, into, T(beta), p.Labels, b * GroupsOf(role), p.Target, b * n)
    }
}

# the buffer population i is read from: the one the sweep writes where a Gauss-Seidel sweep has swept it already
fn (e Engine<T>&) bufferOf(i I64) I64 { return 1 - e.cur if e.fresh[i] else e.cur }

# the step of phase ph: the system's, shortened in a phase nudged toward its targets (settling.md 1.2), times
# 1 - damping
fn (e Engine<T>&) step(ph I64, damping F64) F64 {
    beta := e.Beta[ph]
    dt := e.Dt if beta <= 0.0 else e.Dt / (1.0 + beta * e.ForceSlope())
    return dt * (1.0 - damping)
}

# the step of phase ph as a sweep takes it (dyn/mirror's, on the GPU)
fn (e Engine<T>&) PhaseStep(ph I64, damping F64) F64 { return e.step(ph, damping) }

# the largest self-slope of a readout's force, per unit of beta
fn (e Engine<T>&) ForceSlope() F64 {
    worst F64 = 0.0
    for p in e.Pops {
        role := p.Role
        if role == neuron.Role.Readout: worst = worst.Max(neuron.ForceSlope(role.cost))
    }
    return worst
}

# the largest coupling of a readout's force between its units, per unit of beta, through its activation (in the sup
# and the Euclidean norm alike; neuron.ForceCoupling)
fn (e Engine<T>&) ForceCoupling() F64 {
    worst F64 = 0.0
    for p in e.Pops {
        role := p.Role
        if role == neuron.Role.Readout: worst = worst.Max(neuron.ForceCoupling(role.cost) * neuron.Slope(p.Model))
    }
    return worst
}

# rows r0 .. r1 of a rate (or custom) population stepped from buffer cur into the other; gives their residual
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) rateStep(p mut Population<T>&, r0 I64, r1 I64) T {
    model := p.Model
    if model != neuron.Neuron.Custom: return e.builtStep(p, r0, r1)
    b := p.rate(1 - e.cur)
    if p.Retired > 0: rest(p, p.Total, r0, r1, false)
    worst := custom(model.d, p, b, r0, r1, e.step(r0 / e.Batch, e.damping))
    if p.Retired > 0: retire(p, b, r0, r1)
    return worst
}

# a built-in rate model's rows: the residual and the step, the retired units held at rest
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) builtStep(p mut Population<T>&, r0 I64, r1 I64) T {
    model := p.Model
    if model != neuron.Neuron.Rate: unreachable "a spiking population steps by lifStep"
    a := p.rate(e.cur)
    b := p.rate(1 - e.cur)
    n := p.Size
    if p.Retired > 0: rest(p, a.U, r0, r1, true)
    worst := neuron.RateStep(model.rho, a.U, p.Total, b.U, b.S, r0 * n, r1 * n, T(e.step(r0 / e.Batch, e.damping)))
    if p.Retired > 0: retire(p, b, r0, r1)
    return worst
}

# a custom model's rows: Relax on the drive (the totals), its activity copied into the buffer the sweep writes
fn custom<T>(d mut any neuron.Dynamics&, p mut Population<T>&, b mut Columns<neuron.RateCell<T>>&, r0 I64, r1 I64, dt F64) T {
    match T {
        case F32 {
            drive := tensor.Tensor<F32>.Over(p.Total, 0, p.Total.Len() / p.Size, p.Size)!
            worst := d.Relax(drive, dt, r0, r1)
            activity := d.Activity()
            act := activity.Flat()!
            for i in range r0 * p.Size, r1 * p.Size: b.S[i] = act[i]
            return T(worst)
        }
        case _ { unreachable "a custom model runs in a System<F32>" }
    }
}

# the retired units of rows r0 .. r1 left out of the residual: their totals set to their potentials (held, built-in
# models), or to zero (a custom model's drive) - a retired unit is at rest, so it never keeps a settle from converging
fn rest<T>(p mut Population<T>&, u Array<T>&, r0 I64, r1 I64, held Bool) {
    for r in range r0, r1 {
        for j in range p.Size {
            if p.Alive[j]: continue
            i := r * p.Size + j
            p.Total[i] = u[i] if held else T(0)
        }
    }
}

# the retired units of rows r0 .. r1 held at rest
fn retire<T>(p mut Population<T>&, b mut Columns<neuron.RateCell<T>>&, r0 I64, r1 I64) {
    for r in range r0, r1 {
        for j in range p.Size {
            if p.Alive[j]: continue
            b.U[r * p.Size + j] = T(0)
            b.S[r * p.Size + j] = T(0)
        }
    }
}

# rows r0 .. r1 of a spiking population: a time step from buffer cur into the other, and at a window's end the rates
# and their change (the residual; nothing, zero, before then)
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) lifStep(p mut Population<T>&, r0 I64, r1 I64) T {
    a := p.lif(e.cur)
    b := p.lif(1 - e.cur)
    lif := p.Model
    if lif != neuron.Neuron.Lif: unreachable "a rate population steps by rateStep"
    n := p.Size
    follow := T((4.0 / F64(lif.window)).Min(1.0))
    nudged := p.Force.Len() > 0 and e.Beta[r0 / e.Batch] != 0.0
    for r in range r0, r1 {
        b0 := (r % e.Batch) * n
        neuron.LifStep(a, b, p.Total, p.Const, b0, p.Force, nudged, r * n, (r + 1) * n, T(lif.leak), T(lif.threshold), T(lif.synapse), follow)
        if p.Retired > 0: retireLif(p, b, r)
    }
    if (e.sweep + 1) % lif.window != 0: return T(0)
    return neuron.LifWindow(b, r0 * n, r1 * n, lif.window)
}

fn retireLif<T>(p mut Population<T>&, b mut Columns<neuron.LifCell<T>>&, r I64) {
    for j in range p.Size {
        if p.Alive[j]: continue
        i := r * p.Size + j
        b.V[i] = T(0)
        b.Spike[i] = T(0)
        b.Count[i] = T(0)
    }
}

# ---- the settle ----

# phases lo .. hi settled together from their committed state, within budget: sweeps until every residual is at most
# b.Tol (a spiking system's: at a window's end), committing the state reached either way. fold: the constants are
# made afresh (a nudged phase keeps the free phase's)
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) Settle(lo I64, hi I64, b Budget, fold Bool) Settlement {
    e.prepare(lo, hi, fold)
    e.seidel = b.Scheme.GaussSeidel and not e.Spiking
    e.stopping = e.mayStop(b, lo, hi)
    e.plan(lo, hi)
    e.damping = b.Damping
    e.cur = 0
    e.acc.Kept, e.acc.Slot, e.acc.Started = 0, 0, false
    packs := e.PackAhead and b.Sweeps >= 2 and e.Batch > conn.PackedRows
    if packs { for l in e.Links: l.Repack() }
    out := e.spikes(b) if e.Spiking else e.relax(b, lo * e.Batch, hi * e.Batch, e.depthFor(b))
    if packs { for l in e.Links: l.Unpack() }
    e.seidel = false
    e.stopping = false
    if e.cur == 1: e.commit(lo * e.Batch, hi * e.Batch)
    e.cur = 0
    e.Sweeps += out.Sweeps
    e.Last = out
    return out
}

# a rate system's sweeps over rows r0 .. r1, accelerated by Anderson's method of depth m (0: none): the residual of
# state k measured as the step to k + 1 is made; the first state within the tolerance is the one kept (Gauss-Seidel:
# the state its sweep wrote)
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) relax(b Budget, r0 I64, r1 I64, m I64) Settlement {
    rows := b.Euclid and e.builtIn()
    e.answers.K = b.Stable.Max(1)
    if b.Stable > 0: e.answers.Clear(r0, r1)
    for k in range b.Sweeps + 1 {
        e.sweep = k
        worst := F64(e.sweepOnce())
        e.Skipped += e.idle
        if rows and worst == worst: worst = e.rowsResidual(r0, r1)
        out := Settlement(k, worst, worst <= b.Tol)
        if e.stopping and not out.Converged and worst == worst and e.stopRows(r0, r1, b.Tol, k): e.plan(r0 / e.Batch, r1 / e.Batch)
        if out.Converged and e.seidel: e.cur = 1 - e.cur
        if not out.Converged and b.Stable > 0 and worst == worst and e.answered(r0, r1, k) {
            out.Halted = true
            return out
        }
        if out.Converged or out.Diverged() or k == b.Sweeps: return out
        if m > 0: e.accelerate(r0, r1, worst, m)
        e.cur = 1 - e.cur
    }
    unreachable
}

# after sweep k: each row's answer read from the state the sweep wrote - every readout group's largest unit - and
# recorded (Budget.Stable); a row whose answer has held for the budget's sweeps stops where rows may stop on their own,
# as stopRows does. Whether every row of r0 .. r1 has stopped, or holds its answer
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) answered(r0 I64, r1 I64, k I64) Bool {
    all := true
    stopped := false
    for r in range r0, r1 {
        if e.stopping and e.RowDone[r]: continue
        g I64 = 0
        for p in e.Pops {
            if p.Role != neuron.Role.Readout: continue
            groups := GroupsOf(p.Role).Max(1)
            n := p.Size / groups
            for q in range groups {
                e.answers.Mark(r, g, halt.ArgMax(p.readout(1 - e.cur), r * p.Size + q * n, n))
                g += 1
            }
        }
        held := e.answers.Step(r)
        all = all and held
        if not held or not e.stopping: continue
        stopped = true
        e.RowDone[r] = true
        e.RowStop[r] = k
        for p in e.Pops {
            if p.Role != neuron.Role.Input: copyRows(p.rate(1 - e.cur), p.rate(e.cur), r * p.Size, r * p.Size, p.Size)
        }
    }
    if stopped and not all: e.plan(r0 / e.Batch, r1 / e.Batch)
    return all
}

# whether every relaxing population is a built-in rate model (its residual readable from its two buffers)
fn (e Engine<T>&) builtIn() Bool {
    for p in e.Pops {
        model := p.Model
        if p.Role != neuron.Role.Input and model != neuron.Neuron.Rate: return false
    }
    return true
}

# the largest Euclidean norm, over rows r0 .. r1, of a row's residual r = total - u over its relaxing units, read from
# the sweep's step (u' - u) / dt; retired units are at rest and left out (NaN where a residual is)
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) rowsResidual(r0 I64, r1 I64) F64 {
    worst F64 = 0.0
    for r in range r0, r1 {
        if e.stopping and e.RowDone[r]: continue
        inv := 1.0 / e.step(r / e.Batch, e.damping)
        sum F64 = 0.0
        for p in e.Pops {
            if p.Role != neuron.Role.Input: sum += rowSquares(p, e.cur, r, inv)
        }
        e.RowRes[r] = sum.Sqrt()
        worst = neuron.Worse(worst, sum.Sqrt())
    }
    return worst
}

# whether a settle of phases lo .. hi lets rows stop on their own (RowStops): its sweeps are Jacobi's, plain (no
# Anderson), measured per row (Euclid), of built-in rate models - the settle's test is then each row's own residual
# within the tolerance, so a row that passes it is done whatever the others do. Clears the rows' record
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) mayStop(b Budget, lo I64, hi I64) Bool {
    for r in range lo * e.Batch, hi * e.Batch {
        e.RowDone[r] = false
        e.RowStop[r] = -1
    }
    return e.RowStops and b.Euclid and e.builtIn() and not e.Spiking and not e.seidel and e.depthFor(b) == 0
}

# after sweep k (rows r0 .. r1 measured, RowRes): each row not yet done whose residual is within tol stops - the state
# the sweep wrote, one step closer, is the one it keeps, in both buffers, and it leaves the units (plan). Whether any
# row stopped
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) stopRows(r0 I64, r1 I64, tol F64, k I64) Bool {
    stopped := false
    for r in range r0, r1 {
        if e.RowDone[r] or not (e.RowRes[r] <= tol): continue
        stopped = true
        e.RowDone[r] = true
        e.RowStop[r] = k
        for p in e.Pops {
            if p.Role == neuron.Role.Input: continue
            n := p.Size
            copyRows(p.rate(1 - e.cur), p.rate(e.cur), r * n, r * n, n)
        }
    }
    return stopped
}

# the sum over p's live units in row r of ((u' - u) inv)^2, u read from buffer cur and u' from the other
fn rowSquares<T>(p mut Population<T>&, cur I64, r I64, inv F64) F64 {
    a := p.rate(cur)
    b := p.rate(1 - cur)
    n := p.Size
    sum F64 = 0.0
    for j in range n {
        if p.Retired > 0 and not p.Alive[j]: continue
        d := F64(b.U[r * n + j] - a.U[r * n + j]) * inv
        sum += d * d
    }
    return sum
}

# the Anderson depth a settle runs at: the budget's, at most the depth reserved; none where a population is spiking
# or custom (its state is not a rate population's potentials)
fn (e Engine<T>&) depthFor(b Budget) I64 {
    for p in e.Pops {
        model := p.Model
        if p.Role != neuron.Role.Input and model != neuron.Neuron.Rate: return 0
    }
    return e.Depth(b.Scheme)
}

# ---- Anderson acceleration ----

# after a sweep of rows r0 .. r1 with residual worst: the step the sweep made and the state it read join the history,
# and the buffer written becomes the extrapolation x + f - (dX + dF) gamma, gamma the least-squares combination of the
# steps' differences nearest f. The safeguard: where the residual grew, the extrapolated state is dropped with the
# history, and the buffer written becomes the plain sweep's step from the state before it (which the next sweep takes
# without comparing)
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) accelerate(r0 I64, r1 I64, worst F64, m I64) {
    n := e.gather(r0, r1)
    if e.acc.step(n, m, worst, 1.0): e.scatter(r0, r1)
}

# Anderson's method for an iteration outside the settles - a learner's exact adjoint - on the settle's history (the
# room Reserve made covers every unit's rows of a batch): the depth the scheme gives, at most the depth reserved
fn (e Engine<T>&) Depth(scheme Sweeping) I64 { return scheme.Anderson.Min(e.acc.Depth).Max(0) }

# the history forgotten: before a new iteration
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) Restart() { e.acc.Kept, e.acc.Slot, e.acc.Started = 0, 0, false }

# after a step of the iteration from x to next with residual worst, both laid out as a learner's per-unit arrays (a
# population's rows of Batch at its Unit): next becomes the step Anderson's method of depth m takes instead over the
# relaxing populations' rows, where it takes one. Its safeguard is looser than a settle's: the history is dropped only
# where the residual grew tenfold (a linear iteration's residual under Anderson's method is not monotone in the sup
# norm, and dropping it at every rise cost more sweeps than plain ones)
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) Accelerate(x Array<T>&, next mut Array<T>&, worst F64, m I64) {
    a := e.acc
    at I64 = 0
    for p in e.Pops {
        if p.Role == neuron.Role.Input: continue
        for i in range p.Unit * e.Batch, (p.Unit + p.Size) * e.Batch {
            a.X[at] = x[i]
            a.F[at] = next[i] - x[i]
            at++
        }
    }
    if not a.step(at, m, worst, 10.0): return
    at = 0
    for p in e.Pops {
        if p.Role == neuron.Role.Input: continue
        for i in range p.Unit * e.Batch, (p.Unit + p.Size) * e.Batch {
            next[i] = a.X[at]
            at++
        }
    }
}

# a step of depth m over the n values gathered (X the state a step read, F the step it made, worst its residual):
# whether X now holds the next state - the extrapolation, or, where the residual grew past guard times the last (the
# safeguard), the plain step from the state before, the history dropped; false where the plain step stands
fn (a mut History<T>&) step(n I64, m I64, worst F64, guard F64) Bool {
    if a.Started and worst > guard * a.Last {
        a.Kept, a.Slot, a.Started = 0, 0, false
        for i in range n: a.X[i] = a.PX[i] + a.PF[i]
        return true
    }
    if a.Started: a.push(n, m)
    for i in range n {
        a.PX[i] = a.X[i]
        a.PF[i] = a.F[i]
    }
    a.Started = true
    a.Last = worst
    if a.Kept == 0: return false
    singular := false
    try a.solve(n, m) catch { singular = true }
    if singular {
        a.Kept, a.Slot = 0, 0
        return false
    }
    a.extrapolate(n)
    return true
}

# rows r0 .. r1 of every relaxing population gathered: the potentials read (X) and the step to those written (F);
# gives how many
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) gather(r0 I64, r1 I64) I64 {
    a := e.acc
    at I64 = 0
    for p in e.Pops {
        if p.Role == neuron.Role.Input: continue
        x := p.rate(e.cur)
        y := p.rate(1 - e.cur)
        for i in range r0 * p.Size, r1 * p.Size {
            a.X[at] = x.U[i]
            a.F[at] = y.U[i] - x.U[i]
            at++
        }
    }
    return at
}

# the extrapolated potentials (X) put into the buffer the sweep wrote, its activities from them
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) scatter(r0 I64, r1 I64) {
    a := e.acc
    at I64 = 0
    for p in e.Pops {
        if p.Role == neuron.Role.Input: continue
        model := p.Model
        rho := model.rho if model == neuron.Neuron.Rate else neuron.Rho.Linear
        b := p.rate(1 - e.cur)
        lo, hi := r0 * p.Size, r1 * p.Size
        for i in range lo, hi {
            b.U[i] = a.X[at]
            at++
        }
        neuron.ActivateRun(rho, b.U, b.S, lo, hi)
    }
}

# the differences from the last state and step written into the history's next column (n long), and its Gram row
fn (a mut History<T>&) push(n I64, m I64) {
    c := a.Slot
    for i in range n {
        a.DX[c * n + i] = a.X[i] - a.PX[i]
        a.DF[c * n + i] = a.F[i] - a.PF[i]
    }
    a.Slot = (c + 1) % m
    a.Kept = (a.Kept + 1).Min(m)
    for j in range a.Kept {
        d := dot(a.DF, c * n, a.DF, j * n, n)
        a.Gram[c * m + j] = d
        a.Gram[j * m + c] = d
    }
}

# gamma (in G) from the normal equations (dF^T dF + mu I) gamma = dF^T f, mu a relative 1e-10 of the diagonal; false
# an error where they are singular
fn (a mut History<T>&) solve(n I64, m I64) ? Singular {
    k := a.Kept
    top := 0.0
    for i in range k {
        for j in range k: a.A[i * k + j] = a.Gram[i * m + j]
        a.G[i] = dot(a.DF, i * n, a.F, 0, n)
        top = top.Max(a.Gram[i * m + i])
    }
    for i in range k: a.A[i * k + i] += 1e-10 * top
    try eliminate(a.A, a.G, k)
}

# X = X + F - sum_j gamma_j (DX_j + DF_j): the next state
fn (a mut History<T>&) extrapolate(n I64) {
    for i in range n: a.X[i] += a.F[i]
    for j in range a.Kept {
        g := T(a.G[j])
        at := j * n
        for i in range n: a.X[i] -= g * (a.DX[at + i] + a.DF[at + i])
    }
}

# the sum of a[ao + i] b[bo + i] over n, in F64
fn dot<T>(a Array<T>&, ao I64, b Array<T>&, bo I64, n I64) F64 {
    s := 0.0
    for i in range n: s += F64(a[ao + i]) * F64(b[bo + i])
    return s
}

# normal equations with a zero or non-finite pivot
type Singular error { Pivot }

# a x = b solved in place (x into b) by Gaussian elimination with partial pivoting, k unknowns; an error where a pivot
# is zero or not finite
fn eliminate(a mut Array<F64>&, b mut Array<F64>&, k I64) ? Singular {
    for c in range k {
        p := c
        for i in range c + 1, k: p = i if a[i * k + c].Abs() > a[p * k + c].Abs() else p
        if not (a[p * k + c].Abs() > 0.0 and a[p * k + c].Abs() <= F64.Max): error Singular.Pivot
        swapRows(a, b, k, c, p)
        for i in range c + 1, k {
            f := a[i * k + c] / a[c * k + c]
            for j in range c, k: a[i * k + j] -= f * a[c * k + j]
            b[i] -= f * b[c]
        }
    }
    for c := k - 1, c >= 0, c-- {
        for j in range c + 1, k: b[c] -= a[c * k + j] * b[j]
        b[c] /= a[c * k + c]
    }
}

fn swapRows(a mut Array<F64>&, b mut Array<F64>&, k I64, c I64, p I64) {
    if p == c: return
    for j in range k {
        t := a[c * k + j]
        a[c * k + j] = a[p * k + j]
        a[p * k + j] = t
    }
    t := b[c]
    b[c] = b[p]
    b[p] = t
}

# a spiking system's time steps, its rates' change measured at the end of each window
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) spikes(b Budget) Settlement {
    out := Settlement()
    steps := b.Sweeps.Max(e.Window)
    for k in range steps {
        e.sweep = k
        worst := F64(e.sweepOnce())
        e.cur = 1 - e.cur
        if (k + 1) % e.Window != 0: continue
        out = Settlement(k + 1, worst, worst <= b.Tol)
        if out.Converged or out.Diverged(): return out
    }
    return out
}

# ---- the coupling's spectral norm ----

# the largest singular value of W, the coupling between relaxing units (every connection not folded, a reciprocal one
# both ways), by power iteration on W^T W: sigma = |W v| for the unit v, v <- W^T W v normalized, until sigma moves by
# at most tol relative or after iterations; 0 where nothing couples. Warm-started: v is kept in the engine and the next
# call starts from it, so after a small change of the weights (a lesson's step) a few iterations do. Each iterate is a
# lower bound (|W v| <= sigma for a unit v). Its vectors are laid out by Ready: it allocates nothing
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) Spectral(iterations I64, tol F64) F64 {
    if norm(e.spec) == 0.0: e.specDraw()
    v, w := e.spec, e.specW
    sigma := 0.0
    for _ in range iterations {
        if norm(v) == 0.0: return 0.0
        normalize(v)
        e.couple(v, w, false)
        next := norm(w)
        e.couple(w, v, true)
        moved := (next - sigma).Abs()
        sigma = next
        if moved <= tol * sigma: break
    }
    return sigma
}

# Spectral's vector drawn afresh (seeded: the same start for the same structure), zero on the inputs
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) specDraw() {
    r := rand.Rand(1)
    for i in range e.spec.Len() {
        a := e.spec[i]
        if e.Pops[i].Role == neuron.Role.Input: continue
        for j in range a.Len(): a[j] = T(r.Uniform(-1.0, 1.0))
    }
}

# to = W from (trans: W^T from), every population's vector
fn (e mut Engine<T>&) couple(from mut Vec<mut Array<T>&>&, to mut Vec<mut Array<T>&>&, trans Bool) {
    for a in to {
        for j in range a.Len(): a[j] = T(0)
    }
    ws, sc := e.ws[0], e.sc[0]
    for l in e.Links {
        if l.Folded: continue
        into := l.Pre if trans else l.Post
        out := l.Post if trans else l.Pre
        l.Carry(ws, sc, to[into], from[out], trans)
        if l.Both: l.Carry(ws, sc, to[out], from[into], not trans)
    }
}

# the Euclidean norm of every population's vector together
fn norm<T>(v Vec<mut Array<T>&>&) F64 {
    s := 0.0
    for a in v {
        for x in a: s += F64(x) * F64(x)
    }
    return s.Sqrt()
}

# the vectors scaled to norm 1 (left where they are zero)
fn normalize<T>(v mut Vec<mut Array<T>&>&) {
    n := norm(v)
    if not (n > 0.0): return
    for a in v {
        for j in range a.Len(): a[j] = T(F64(a[j]) / n)
    }
}

# ---- what must not allocate, decided while compiling (S17a, G42) ----
# Pinned: every step of a population, the state's moves between settles and phases, the units' plan, an input's closed
# form - and a whole settle (rate or spiking, every connection's products, in tasks or not, a custom model through
# `any Dynamics&`, whose methods promise `noalloc`): gemm's workspaces are reserved at Ready and never grown, a sparse
# product's join and a sweep's join allocate nothing. Resident memory over many settles is measured too (system.olang).

fn builtStepOf(e mut Engine<F32>&, p mut Population<F32>&) F32 { return e.builtStep(p, 0, 1) }
fn restOf(p mut Population<F32>&) { rest(p, p.Total, 0, 1, true) }
fn lifStepOf(e mut Engine<F32>&, p mut Population<F32>&) F32 { return e.lifStep(p, 0, 1) }
fn resetOf(e mut Engine<F32>&) { e.Reset(0) }
fn warmOf(e mut Engine<F32>&) { e.Warm(1, 0) }
fn commitOf(e mut Engine<F32>&) { e.commit(0, 1) }
fn planOf(e mut Engine<F32>&) { e.plan(0, 1) }
fn settleOf(e mut Engine<F32>&, b Budget) F64 { return e.Settle(0, e.Batch, b, true).Residual }
fn settle64Of(e mut Engine<F64>&, b Budget) F64 { return e.Settle(0, e.Batch, b, false).Residual }
fn closedFormOf(e mut Engine<F32>&, p mut Population<F32>&) {
    e.closedForm(p, 0, 1)
    e.clearCounts(p, 0, 1)
}

assert not builtStepOf.Allocates, "a rate population's step allocates"
assert not restOf.Allocates, "holding retired units out of the residual allocates"
assert not lifStepOf.Allocates, "a spiking population's step allocates"
assert not resetOf.Allocates, "a cold start allocates"
assert not warmOf.Allocates, "a warm start allocates"
assert not commitOf.Allocates, "committing a settle allocates"
assert not planOf.Allocates, "a settle's units allocate"
assert not closedFormOf.Allocates, "an input's closed form allocates"
assert not settleOf.Allocates, "a settle allocates"
assert not settle64Of.Allocates, "a settle in F64 allocates"
